[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186616971 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dad1bd8-9142-5eb8-b7f0-b33771f477d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186616449} {:answer/id #uuid "56e911d8-e059-5da2-9177-44a840733abd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186616525} {:answer/id #uuid "9125514a-353b-5464-9049-fc8b1df8a77f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186616631} {:answer/id #uuid "97fddb80-d9bb-5913-95a9-c6c536108d31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186616650} {:answer/id #uuid "f3118812-15d4-5ff3-82cf-309a67010bfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186616800}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3118812-15d4-5ff3-82cf-309a67010bfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186616800} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e5ebbda-10a4-5c6f-bde5-285a3f82ac66" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186616854}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "559f010f-9577-5213-8738-e1be610cbaa9" :question/math-academy-id "q-152131" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{\\infty }\\frac{18}{36+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{k}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{k}\\right)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{\\infty }\\frac{18}{36+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t & =18\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{a}\\frac{1}{36+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =18\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{a}\\frac{1}{{6}^{2}+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =18\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{6}\\right)]}_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{a} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{6}\\right)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{6}\\right)-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{6}\\right)]-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{6}\\right),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{6}\\right)]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{6}\\right)]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/fa/fa90489ad28c3fde493a83b61ef040867d575ad06c989439de9764d0d23b002e.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{\\infty }\\frac{18}{36+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{6}\\right)]-\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186616976 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45cde212-fa9b-5919-9113-bbed84631552" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186616488}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45cde212-fa9b-5919-9113-bbed84631552" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186616488} :answer-field/id #uuid "820efab7-cac1-52a9-9067-be74122c9ddf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186616883}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "665bdbda-051e-5fe1-ac13-78ba82378fe4" :question/math-academy-id "q-351315" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/5}\\frac{20}{1+25{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+(kx)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(kx)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/5}\\frac{20}{1+25{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =20\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1/5}\\frac{1}{1+25{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =20\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1/5}\\frac{1}{1+(5x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =20\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5x)]}_{a}^{1/5} \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5a)) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{4}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5a)]).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5x)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5x)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/da/daa942ec89fba177dfa505e127f0471e1c6309d909c6312b1694ccf898750879.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/5}\\frac{20}{1+25{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{4}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5a)]) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =3\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186616982 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e55b4572-dd73-58bd-9322-d6b22db68cdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi}{48}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186616779}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e55b4572-dd73-58bd-9322-d6b22db68cdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi}{48}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186616779} :answer-field/id #uuid "09dce933-cbde-517c-956b-73b70837fad5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186616830}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "764603f5-2284-55fa-941f-4f8a2a7da0b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-351754" :question/problem "Use the substitution $u={x}^{6}$ to evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{x}^{5}}{1+16{x}^{12}}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{x}^{5}}{1+16{x}^{12}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={x}^{6}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6{x}^{5}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{d}u={x}^{5}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty$ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{6}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\infty$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{x}^{5}}{1+16{x}^{12}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{\\infty }^{0}\\frac{1}{1+16{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{1+16{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{x}^{5}}{1+16{x}^{12}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{1+16{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{1}{1+16{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4u)]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{24}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4u)]_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{24}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4a)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{24}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4a)]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4x)]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4x)]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/ee/eef6460d2f20f1f1a4fb1390411d9a4e97f7e8025d432428f2c51d4777cc11c2.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{x}^{5}}{1+16{x}^{12}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{24}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4a)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{24}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{48}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186616988 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a6e0eca-67be-56b9-8652-74bed767311c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x+y\\ne 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186616394} {:answer/id #uuid "10b607ae-1eff-534c-b675-a0d77d5c2bcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x+y>1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186616408} {:answer/id #uuid "6eea5ab4-b4e4-5d8a-b1f9-554b949e310c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x+y<1\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,x+y\\ne 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186616568} {:answer/id #uuid "8d190156-db74-5c1a-af0f-a94a98a55a4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x+y>0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186616621} {:answer/id #uuid "aaf05115-4d6a-50b8-94a3-32c309898394" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x+y>0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,x+y\\ne 1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186616686}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aaf05115-4d6a-50b8-94a3-32c309898394" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x+y>0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,x+y\\ne 1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186616686} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd27d978-3a1e-5ebc-bc9f-a8e1b8f0ae0f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186616914}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "80f7423d-3482-56f5-827a-abaa51f94af1" :question/math-academy-id "q-132648" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)},$ find the set of values $(x,y)$ on which $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ exists." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y)$ is defined only when \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & >0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y) & \\ne 0\\,⟹\\,x+y\\ne 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x+y>0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,x+y\\ne 1\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(x+y){ln}^{2}⁡(x+y)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ is defined only for \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & >0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y) & \\ne 0\\,⟹\\,x+y\\ne 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n So, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x+y>0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,x+y\\ne 1\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}} & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x+y>0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,x+y\\ne 1\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x+y>0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,x+y\\ne 1\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x+y>0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,x+y\\ne 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186617030 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7999dd6-8313-501d-a057-a5f2d1d8e4cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186616680}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7999dd6-8313-501d-a057-a5f2d1d8e4cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186616680} :answer-field/id #uuid "512bc5f8-32fd-5825-ad5b-923eef556867" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186616857}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eefe5685-79a9-5120-bd37-2f7571a763c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-362162" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{k}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{k}\\right)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0) \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{2}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/31/319d7a909eb214a44690c374ab83fc5b46c531018d82583bc7b8782e2283b13f.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186617774 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14af4d6e-fc18-5955-94b0-04c27bf8c302" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617284} {:answer/id #uuid "4fe313bb-93c2-5a29-b987-8ef805da0432" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617398} {:answer/id #uuid "7eba1420-1df2-5dbb-9890-2b9a02433835" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{3}^{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617455} {:answer/id #uuid "ab5c2018-63aa-5261-932a-ef2f0dc6bf15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617528} {:answer/id #uuid "aff6bafc-b3c5-5a31-9439-5556897c9573" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{3}^{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617537}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14af4d6e-fc18-5955-94b0-04c27bf8c302" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617284} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9b262fa-9f74-58a6-bb9d-49b5e143cc93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617754}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "058a4daf-4885-5fa6-95cc-21e80d15d3bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-100393" :question/problem "${({3}^{-4})}^{3}=$" :question/worked-solution "To raise a power to another power, we simply multiply the exponents:\n\n$$\n{({3}^{-4})}^{3}={3}^{(-4)\\cdot 3}={3}^{-12}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate ${3}^{-12},$ we find the reciprocal of the base $(3)$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the opposite sign:\n\n$$\n{3}^{-12}={\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{12}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186617783 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0de37584-4bb3-5aca-a724-52bc5be8f33f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617276} {:answer/id #uuid "93098f6d-3ee1-5adc-801a-5441680dc5ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=-\\frac{21}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617490} {:answer/id #uuid "a318da04-0d14-58b9-acdb-71a6ee2f2492" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617513} {:answer/id #uuid "bdf33e00-91e9-5033-8cc1-ed2c720cd188" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617563} {:answer/id #uuid "f18797bb-19fa-588a-b1d9-a90ffc3785d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617648}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bdf33e00-91e9-5033-8cc1-ed2c720cd188" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617563} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a12af45-f34d-5225-817d-989853cdd484" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617703}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1779725b-3fcf-5367-a3e9-26ad9e574efd" :question/math-academy-id "q-60353" :question/problem "The equation $2{x}^{2}-6x-5=0$ can be written in the form $2(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $p+q.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-6x-5 & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-6x & =5 \\\\ 2({x}^{2}-3x) & =5 \\\\ 2[{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x+{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}-{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}] & =5 \\\\ 2[{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}] & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{9}{4} & =5 \\\\ 2{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{2} & =5 \\\\ 2{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =5+\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ 2{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{19}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-\\frac{3}{2}$, $q=\\frac{19}{2}.$ Finally,\n\n$$\np+q=\\frac{19}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}=\\frac{16}{2}=8.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186617785 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c3499da-8cb4-5ab0-a38d-17c492370352" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617271} {:answer/id #uuid "37a24197-bb49-5fe3-a439-8f6029051397" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617359} {:answer/id #uuid "96fc41e0-a931-5299-b320-b819e37f0217" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617495} {:answer/id #uuid "b25b03a9-a2ae-57cb-b586-005c3f77e077" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617538} {:answer/id #uuid "c1b930f2-dfcf-561d-ae99-0a7886f9b79d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617573}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b25b03a9-a2ae-57cb-b586-005c3f77e077" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617538} :answer-field/id #uuid "c77127a0-b9dc-5211-b535-33dd262efdbe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617743}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1b4091f0-4a95-54e5-98db-b30b1df1c475" :question/math-academy-id "q-82909" :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x+2y,\\,v=y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(x+2y)^{4}-y=0$ and  the line $y=1.$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+2y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nSubstituting $x+2y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2y)^{4}-y &=0\\,⟹\\,{u}^{4}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v={u}^{4},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &=1\\,⟹\\,v=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v &={u}^{4} \\\\ v &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u &=\\pm 1 \\\\ v &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,{u}^{4}\\le v\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/21/21a418b848dc981a9490ca72038a077205d79cf152b00581c5bc6eb7f48269a9.png)\n\n   \n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|1|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[{\\int }_{{u}^{4}}^{1}\\,dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}v{|}_{{u}^{4}}^{1}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1-{u}^{4})du \\\\  & ={[u-\\frac{{u}^{5}}{5}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =2-\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186617786 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c8f2dae-7d6c-5bac-a066-037fb4b69462" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{6}^{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617367} {:answer/id #uuid "7ee138b0-db82-50c7-ba2f-52700e4d873e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}^{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617457} {:answer/id #uuid "825d42c9-2a74-5c5c-b60b-c3f1ba9b7de4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617465} {:answer/id #uuid "f10f3d38-507c-51bf-9597-7619e5568f99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}^{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 1759218661764