b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{-8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617488} {:answer/id #uuid "aa46ba5e-d883-59a1-be7b-4856ffda79da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617524} {:answer/id #uuid "d9cd7b88-db1e-5f48-acb8-5b4f5276cbd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617609}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa46ba5e-d883-59a1-be7b-4856ffda79da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617524} :answer-field/id #uuid "5eca275e-ebdd-581b-ad82-f7573912474b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617695}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "559677bd-eaa2-5320-b61d-b6cbe819848f" :question/math-academy-id "q-141302" :question/problem "${({\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{-5})}^{-3}=$" :question/worked-solution "To raise a power to another power, we simply multiply the exponents:\n\n$$\n{({\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{-5})}^{-3}={\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{(-5)\\cdot (-3)}={\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{15}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186617817 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06a5311b-b490-5a09-85f8-fc1b269f1205" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617256} {:answer/id #uuid "8ccf1135-1fe5-583b-b4ee-593321640eb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617482} {:answer/id #uuid "918803c3-671a-5e4a-8563-3e9619b2e943" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617489} {:answer/id #uuid "9b07c076-1613-5f0e-bb39-6116510a9e65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617500} {:answer/id #uuid "e3d82748-249b-5512-b9b1-28fc80d6c9ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617629}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06a5311b-b490-5a09-85f8-fc1b269f1205" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617256} :answer-field/id #uuid "112a4813-b58b-57f9-bd0c-7e592f6fe194" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617673}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "66c2a1bc-864c-53e1-9f03-760ac753becb" :question/math-academy-id "q-160851" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the following lines.\n\n$$\ny=3x,\\,y=3x-4,\\,x=y-1,\\,x=y+2\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe'll assume this transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv,\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in five steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the equations of our curves as follows:\n\n$$\n3x-y=0,\\,3x-y=4,\\,x-y=-1,\\,x-y=2\n$$\n\nThis suggests the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nu=3x-y,\\,v=x-y\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=3x-y$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 3x-y=0\\,⟹\\,u=0 \\\\  & 3x-y=4\\,⟹\\,u=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=x-y$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & x-y=-1\\,⟹\\,v=-1 \\\\  & x-y=2\\,⟹\\,v=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 4,\\,-1\\le v\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/ff/ff0564b19ac1a55b0bc5d50a8b93bf0a6196a92c388d95a82bccf74039243e05.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3+1 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{∬}_{Δ}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (4-0)\\cdot (2-(-1)) \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186617821 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1011087d-276b-576a-b19b-40b66248e9b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{8}^{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617281} {:answer/id #uuid "1c53c9ad-d2df-567c-9e57-bc39fb68e673" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{8}^{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617297} {:answer/id #uuid "8a9fb3d6-7430-5555-8dd9-0d2e57a44403" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{8}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617478} {:answer/id #uuid "ba652b55-2969-50fe-a106-97ca92197684" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617554} {:answer/id #uuid "bf07d70c-e067-5295-a801-8206db108400" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)}^{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617566}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c53c9ad-d2df-567c-9e57-bc39fb68e673" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{8}^{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617297} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5f5e2ff-0ad6-5cb2-854e-406ca47cf85c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617741}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7563aa9b-c648-5a2d-9c45-17b029811f83" :question/math-academy-id "q-141308" :question/problem "${({\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)}^{-2})}^{5}=$" :question/worked-solution "To raise a power to another power, we simply multiply the exponents:\n\n$$\n{({\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)}^{-2})}^{5}={\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)}^{(-2)\\cdot 5}={\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)}^{-10}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate ${\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)}^{-10},$ we find the reciprocal of the base $\\frac{1}{8}$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the opposite sign:\n\n$$\n{\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)}^{-10}={8}^{10}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186617823 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f22a2a1-6c74-5011-88a3-6593201fbaac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617304} {:answer/id #uuid "3a38df03-fd47-564e-b911-347a45274d09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617364} {:answer/id #uuid "65ffd847-dfd9-5dba-b54b-a42b340f9418" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{-10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617422} {:answer/id #uuid "89bfa427-9e6e-50d9-a893-30ecf2a09fff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{-6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617476} {:answer/id #uuid "c9ab22b5-224e-5171-aa27-4cba249779f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617583}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a38df03-fd47-564e-b911-347a45274d09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617364} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc0cd124-6543-53c9-867e-0f58d9b70d03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617762}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7887fabe-40ee-5e52-83ed-43a8fae2321b" :question/math-academy-id "q-141305" :question/problem "${({\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{-2})}^{-8}=$" :question/worked-solution "To raise a power to another power, we simply multiply the exponents:\n\n$$\n{({\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{-2})}^{-8}={\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{(-2)\\cdot (-8)}={\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{16}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186617824 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "067db027-125f-563d-954d-9e67b4fde190" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,2x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617255} {:answer/id #uuid "34ca402b-b080-5fdc-883f-f6ad834bfbc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,8x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617347} {:answer/id #uuid "896ef29a-68f5-587c-8d99-31ed6c6870ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,4x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617474} {:answer/id #uuid "c0646f5d-df70-507c-bc50-a604aa30aa73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,-4x-2y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617568} {:answer/id #uuid "e0c6f33c-e850-5204-a0a0-b42ca512f797" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,8x+2y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617620}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "896ef29a-68f5-587c-8d99-31ed6c6870ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,4x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617474} :answer-field/id #uuid "84a6c8a4-4db1-5fa0-a4bf-6c47d6502129" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617714}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "852b8c26-61ec-5ce4-909a-b99005179ddb" :question/math-academy-id "q-162362" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the half-ellipsoid $z=\\sqrt{9-4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ and the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ Given that the curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above and $\\text{curl}\\,F=⟨z,\\,0,\\,0⟩,$ the circulation of $F$ along $C$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the half-ellipsoid $z=\\sqrt{9-4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ and the cylinder. One such surface is our half-ellipsoid $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=\\sqrt{9-4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\sqrt{9-4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}=\\frac{-4x}{\\sqrt{9-4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\sqrt{9-4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}=\\frac{-y}{\\sqrt{9-4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨z,\\,0,\\,0⟩{|}_{z=\\sqrt{9-4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =⟨\\sqrt{9-4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}},\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{\\sqrt{9-4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}_{P},\\,\\underbrace{0}_{Q},\\,\\underbrace{0}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-\\sqrt{9-4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{4x}{\\sqrt{9-4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\right)-0\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{9-4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\right)+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4x\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/ea/eae72066363dd2c749f85fcc1568c15d65d193af83b9185ab084aef9a38db045.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}4x\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,4x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186617832 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0460466a-2a60-56f8-bae5-fb1512f14dba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617248} {:answer/id #uuid "39efb9dd-5f7f-5652-9ec9-92032719bbc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617363} {:answer/id #uuid "ac17b3b8-4780-50df-b753-e377ccc0e225" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617529} {:answer/id #uuid "bc181004-2175-513a-80eb-0d3f60dec0c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{1/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617558} {:answer/id #uuid "cbaac5b2-5467-5d19-a4f9-ea8bc5a5b599" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617588}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac17b3b8-4780-50df-b753-e377ccc0e225" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617529} :answer-field/id #uuid "40311ab3-e89e-50fa-ac3e-5666b5f4a0c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617687}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9f12c85a-f1ce-56e6-92b6-60d43e903dab" :question/math-academy-id "q-141290" :question/problem "${({\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{6})}^{3}=$" :question/worked-solution "To raise a power to another power, we simply multiply the exponents:\n\n$$\n{({\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{6})}^{3}={\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{6\\cdot 3}={\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{18}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186617841 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00932f26-2b47-5ff3-8cb5-8aa2c89e710a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617242} {:answer/id #uuid "2d870863-1d12-5745-89bf-ae892441530f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617334} {:answer/id #uuid "9db1417d-fd82-5b18-9bc3-3ba28317f539" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617505} {:answer/id #uuid "f1ef297f-fb08-5986-83a8-96a66672543e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617649} {:answer/id #uuid "fc685a95-36b1-5083-9821-45c69a790526" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617657}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d870863-1d12-5745-89bf-ae892441530f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617334} :answer-field/id #uuid "078e0dea-6e4c-5a4f-80cf-6cc6c26934d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617669}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "af302994-8f59-5ef6-a68b-2b3888da4fed" :question/math-academy-id "q-160655" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the following curves and lines.\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{x},\\,y=\\frac{5}{x},\\,x={y}^{2},\\,x=4{y}^{2}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe'll assume this transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv,\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in five steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the equations of our curves as follows:\n\n$$\nxy=2,\\,xy=5,\\,\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}=1,\\,\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}=4\n$$\n\nThis suggests the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nu=xy,\\,v=\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=xy$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & xy=2\\,⟹\\,u=2 \\\\  & xy=5\\,⟹\\,u=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}=1\\,⟹\\,v=1 \\\\  & \\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}=4\\,⟹\\,v=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 5,\\,1\\le v\\le 4\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/ff/ff8f8b6af1d1a81a0c7f40b29e92351431a45f67ebc057fd39acd2cd2280f48b.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}y & x \\\\ \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}} & -\\frac{2x}{{y}^{3}}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2x}{{y}^{2}}-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =-3v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{3v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-\\frac{1}{3v}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 3\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186617842 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0df4d76b-d9eb-5cf6-b303-ec66233f4322" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617278} {:answer/id #uuid "21ca45d2-7791-5884-9b4f-8056a9efa6ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617311} {:answer/id #uuid "43ad0f4f-c5ff-5fcb-980a-c8209d1a6fee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617375} {:answer/id #uuid "b2a31807-dc04-5507-b026-744aedff0c21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617540} {:answer/id #uuid "fe288a64-79ba-513a-9f04-9f55a393d4c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617664}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0df4d76b-d9eb-5cf6-b303-ec66233f4322" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617278} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c86b770-099e-5dc6-bd38-a0e45a33cc06" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617706}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b1d5ffaa-6323-5539-b03d-e56382198099" :question/math-academy-id "q-96285" :question/problem "Given that the continuous random variable $W$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{3}{2}],$ compute $P(W\\le 1).$" :question/worked-solution "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $W$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(w)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le w\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur random variable $W$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{3}{2}],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(w)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3},\\,-\\frac{3}{2}\\le w\\le \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W\\le 1) & =P(-\\frac{3}{2}\\le W\\le 1) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3/2}^{1}f(w)\\,\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}w \\\\  & =\\frac{1-\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186617844 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33a47eb6-f2b7-5aaf-a621-372f14930b43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617345} {:answer/id #uuid "39323b63-908b-5c81-8b6f-ec673db43da5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617360} {:answer/id #uuid "50ac51ca-22b4-5df2-a421-fe1f447842c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617400} {:answer/id #uuid "7a88fc97-d0b3-5fd9-844f-1a1e361f132d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617449} {:answer/id #uuid "8caa5414-b802-5170-9c8c-9a050cd7c154" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617481}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8caa5414-b802-5170-9c8c-9a050cd7c154" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617481} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a99a52c-e996-5a20-bc4b-efc83eee630b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617704}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b4c23fa9-f154-5b24-977d-0b5df3576acf" :question/math-academy-id "q-38372" :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=x,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(x+y)^{2}-x=0$ and the line $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=x\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nSubstituting $x+y=u$ and $x=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+y)^{2}-x &=0\\,⟹\\,{u}^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v={u}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhile substituting $x=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x &=2\\,⟹\\,v=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system: \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v &={u}^{2} \\\\ v &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u &=\\pm \\sqrt{2} \\\\ v &=2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm \\sqrt{2},2).$ \n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,{u}^{2}\\le v\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\n![](images/34/349486642eca07c8f1a619cb567dcb578c39a5ff024067a0910301039f3ae1d9.png)\n\n \n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(-1)^{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-1|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}[{\\int }_{{u}^{2}}^{2}\\,dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}[v]_{{u}^{2}}^{2}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}(2-{u}^{2})\\,du \\\\  & ={[2u-\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}]}_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186617854 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17398767-f74a-5484-a86f-509d3dacbc97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617286}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17398767-f74a-5484-a86f-509d3dacbc97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617286} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b045d95-1fc4-5f95-9f52-7acc3c4e2670" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617693} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c102ff21-d181-5fea-9956-e19b647ff91d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3t^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617570}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c102ff21-d181-5fea-9956-e19b647ff91d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3t^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617570} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e244d21-5ce0-52be-aa9d-c2880fce3597" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617712}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cbe5809a-91b5-5133-bff7-c8eb25e7595b" :question/math-academy-id "q-280222" :question/problem "Determine a set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ that generate a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and which passes through the point $(6,-3).$ Express your answer in the form $x=2pt,y=p{t}^{2},$ where $p$ is a constant.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a vertical parabola  that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=2pt,\\,y=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(6,-3),$ we can substitute $x=6$ and $y=-3$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6 &=2pt \\\\ -3 &=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6 & =2pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{3}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3 & =p{\\left(\\frac{3}{p}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ -3 & =p\\cdot \\frac{9}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ -3 & =\\frac{9}{p} \\\\ p & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=2(-3)t,\\,y=(-3){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=-6t,\\,y=-3{t}^{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186617858 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05fe0f8a-63ab-5a02-ad9b-8b23ae4d24ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8s^3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617254}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05fe0f8a-63ab-5a02-ad9b-8b23ae4d24ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8s^3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617254} :answer-field/id #uuid "df8820ee-14ac-594c-8a78-254f87702bfa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617749}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d73ef19b-f3a8-5724-b463-c35b4f95bb4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-168069" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}s}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}$ in terms of $s.$\n\n$\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}s}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find an expression for the second derivative, we differentiate the equation with respect to $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}\\right) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{s}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}s}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{s}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({s}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2s\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =4s\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}$ into the above and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}s}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} & =4s\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =4s(2{s}^{2}) \\\\  & =8{s}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186618126 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d1b1457-863a-5c12-985e-b6e4c0a2e081" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\times \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186618125} {:answer/id #uuid "29a01b52-c0b3-5dd0-9e20-b4d6554c58d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8^{7}\\times \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186618128} {:answer/id #uuid "84961eee-ce4a-51a2-ad25-a06e7d8855b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8^{2}\\times \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186618135} {:answer/id #uuid "acb68d65-7c40-58ea-99c3-47dd5c792d50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8^{8}\\times \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186618137} {:answer/id #uuid "ebd27a18-75e9-5616-a0d4-8414b84f76cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8^{8}\\times -\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186618144}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "acb68d65-7c40-58ea-99c3-47dd5c792d50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8^{8}\\times \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186618137} :answer-field/id #uuid "14a2f7d4-c0a9-54db-be22-09708c12ede9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186618127}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1166796a-d451-5c5b-a736-f47869be2dce" :question/problem "$(8\\times -\\frac{1}{2})^{8}=$" :question/worked-solution "To raise the product of two numbers to the power of $8$, we can raise each number to the power of $8,$ and then multiply:\n\n$$\n(8\\times -\\frac{1}{2})^{8}=(8\\times \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right))^{8}=8^{8}\\times \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{8}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186618130 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f091da1-6da4-5fb5-8eaf-c51547be85a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(-\\frac{1}{7}\\right)^{9}\\times 10^{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186618129} {:answer/id #uuid "7bc2407c-5a17-5226-bed9-0ebf97c35531" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(-\\frac{1}{7}\\right)^{4}\\times 10^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186618134} {:answer/id #uuid "a1330ae6-6cbd-5dbc-835c-4d769bcacc56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(-\\frac{1}{7}\\right)^{2}\\times 10^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186618136} {:answer/id #uuid "ad5e7bd6-dfdb-5599-88c3-0ff6ad411a65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(-\\frac{1}{7}\\right)^{4}\\times 10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186618138} {:answer/id #uuid "d30426d4-f7d3-56